WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.118
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online B two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.418 --> 00:00:08.711
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.011 --> 00:00:14.508
Unit two: Critical Learning. Today you will use noun clauses as the subject of a sentence.

4
00:00:14.808 --> 00:00:17.426
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:21.901
Part one: words. You will learn twelve new words.

6
00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:24.030
Analytical thinking.

7
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:28.990
Analytical thinking means breaking a problem into parts to understand it.

8
00:00:29.583 --> 00:00:32.384
Analytical thinking helps in maths and science.

9
00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:36.228
Your turn. Say: Analytical thinking.

10
00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:40.509
Analytical thinking.

11
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.785
Evaluate evidence.

12
00:00:43.783 --> 00:00:47.223
Evaluate evidence means to judge if facts are strong and true.

13
00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:50.466
Good scientists evaluate evidence carefully.

14
00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:54.538
Your turn. Say: Evaluate evidence.

15
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.098
Evaluate evidence.

16
00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.269
Logical reasoning.

17
00:01:02.283 --> 00:01:06.013
Logical reasoning means thinking step by step to reach a conclusion.

18
00:01:06.617 --> 00:01:09.092
Logical reasoning helped me solve the puzzle.

19
00:01:09.683 --> 00:01:12.777
Your turn. Say: Logical reasoning.

20
00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:17.006
Logical reasoning.

21
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:18.844
Assumption.

22
00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.732
Assumption means something you believe without proof.

23
00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:25.957
Don't make an assumption. Check the facts.

24
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.854
Your turn. Say: Assumption.

25
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:32.713
Assumption.

26
00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.799
Bias awareness.

27
00:01:35.783 --> 00:01:40.093
Bias awareness means knowing that opinions can be unfair or one-sided.

28
00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:43.089
Bias awareness makes you a fairer reader.

29
00:01:43.683 --> 00:01:46.405
Your turn. Say: Bias awareness.

30
00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:50.755
Bias awareness.

31
00:01:51.517 --> 00:01:52.576
Inference.

32
00:01:53.583 --> 00:01:55.954
Inference means a conclusion from clues.

33
00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.304
From her smile, my inference was that she passed.

34
00:01:59.917 --> 00:02:02.534
Your turn. Say: Inference.

35
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:06.459
Inference.

36
00:02:07.217 --> 00:02:08.485
Problem-solving.

37
00:02:09.483 --> 00:02:12.435
Problem-solving means finding ways to fix problems.

38
00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.955
Problem-solving is a key skill for any job.

39
00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:19.121
Your turn. Say: Problem-solving.

40
00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:23.188
Problem-solving.

41
00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:25.369
Interdisciplinary.

42
00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:29.683
Interdisciplinary means using more than one school subject.

43
00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:34.071
Our project is interdisciplinary: science and art.

44
00:02:34.683 --> 00:02:37.591
Your turn. Say: Interdisciplinary.

45
00:02:40.433 --> 00:02:41.887
Interdisciplinary.

46
00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:44.092
Constructive feedback.

47
00:02:45.083 --> 00:02:48.824
Constructive feedback means helpful comments that show how to improve.

48
00:02:49.417 --> 00:02:52.508
My teacher gives constructive feedback on essays.

49
00:02:53.117 --> 00:02:56.094
Your turn. Say: Constructive feedback.

50
00:02:58.933 --> 00:03:00.631
Constructive feedback.

51
00:03:01.383 --> 00:03:02.884
Reflective practice.

52
00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:07.195
Reflective practice means thinking about what you did to learn from it.

53
00:03:07.783 --> 00:03:10.398
Reflective practice makes me a better learner.

54
00:03:10.983 --> 00:03:13.949
Your turn. Say: Reflective practice.

55
00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:18.672
Reflective practice.

56
00:03:19.417 --> 00:03:21.230
Intellectual curiosity.

57
00:03:22.217 --> 00:03:26.329
Intellectual curiosity means a strong wish to learn and understand.

58
00:03:26.917 --> 00:03:29.566
Intellectual curiosity keeps me reading.

59
00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.394
Your turn. Say: Intellectual curiosity.

60
00:03:36.233 --> 00:03:38.256
Intellectual curiosity.

61
00:03:39.017 --> 00:03:40.134
Synthesis.

62
00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:44.520
Synthesis means combining ideas into one new whole.

63
00:03:45.117 --> 00:03:47.720
The essay was a synthesis of five sources.

64
00:03:48.317 --> 00:03:51.004
Your turn. Say: Synthesis.

65
00:03:53.867 --> 00:03:55.030
Synthesis.

66
00:03:55.833 --> 00:03:59.589
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

67
00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.600
Visal, what makes a good learner?

68
00:04:03.717 --> 00:04:07.272
What matters most is how you evaluate evidence.

69
00:04:08.383 --> 00:04:10.209
Why is that so important?

70
00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:17.777
Because what we read online is not always true. We need bias awareness.

71
00:04:18.883 --> 00:04:20.360
And how do you improve?

72
00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:25.922
What helps me is constructive feedback and reflective practice.

73
00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:28.718
Part three: grammar.

74
00:04:29.583 --> 00:04:32.047
Grammar focus: Noun clauses.

75
00:04:32.347 --> 00:04:33.557
Look at the pattern.

76
00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:37.074
What matters most is how you evaluate evidence.

77
00:04:37.983 --> 00:04:39.704
What I learned was very useful.

78
00:04:40.617 --> 00:04:42.686
I don't know why he made that assumption.

79
00:04:43.633 --> 00:04:51.350
A noun clause works like a noun. It can be the subject or the object. Use normal word order: 'what I think', not 'what do I think'.

80
00:04:52.117 --> 00:04:55.187
Be careful! Don't say: I don't know what does he think.

81
00:04:57.133 --> 00:04:58.947
Say: I don't know what he thinks.

82
00:04:59.247 --> 00:05:02.942
A noun clause uses normal word order, not question order.

83
00:05:03.733 --> 00:05:06.328
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

84
00:05:07.133 --> 00:05:08.761
Thinking Like a Scientist.

85
00:05:09.061 --> 00:05:11.966
Critical learners do not accept every assumption.

86
00:05:12.967 --> 00:05:16.336
What they do first is evaluate evidence from good sources.

87
00:05:17.333 --> 00:05:20.088
They use logical reasoning and bias awareness.

88
00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:24.528
Constructive feedback and reflective practice help them improve.

89
00:05:25.533 --> 00:05:27.962
What drives them is intellectual curiosity.

90
00:05:29.067 --> 00:05:33.219
Part five: quiz. Three questions. Are you ready?

91
00:05:34.083 --> 00:05:36.315
What matters most blank how you think.

92
00:05:36.615 --> 00:05:39.595
A, is. B, are. C, be.

93
00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:44.877
The answer is A: is.

94
00:05:46.017 --> 00:05:48.237
A conclusion from clues is an blank.

95
00:05:48.537 --> 00:05:52.376
A, inference. B, assumption. C, synthesis.

96
00:05:55.933 --> 00:05:58.037
The answer is A: inference.

97
00:05:59.183 --> 00:06:02.495
Helpful comments that show how to improve are blank feedback.

98
00:06:02.795 --> 00:06:07.052
A, constructive. B, logical. C, analytical.

99
00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:12.507
The answer is A: constructive.

100
00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:16.944
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

101
00:06:17.783 --> 00:06:20.955
Describe a time you used critical thinking to solve a problem.

102
00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:31.297
Now listen to Visal.

103
00:06:31.597 --> 00:06:42.472
A friend shared a news story that seemed strange. What I did was check two other sources. I found that the story was false, so I told my friend.

104
00:06:42.772 --> 00:06:44.434
Excellent answer, Visal!

105
00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:49.303
Great work! Now you can use twelve new words and noun clauses.

106
00:06:49.603 --> 00:06:54.461
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

107
00:06:54.761 --> 00:06:57.332
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

108
00:06:57.632 --> 00:07:00.075
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

